<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<wml>

  <card id="upload" title="Raw PUT Single-File Upload">

    <!--
      WapForm for Web — <upload> tag demo page

      This page receives an upload request of a "completely different shape" from multiupload.wml; the two are not interchangeable:

        multiupload.wml  <multiupload>  multipart/form-data
                                  what a browser <form enctype="multipart/form-data">
                                  sends: several files at once, each with a field name
                                  and the original file name.

        upload.wml       <upload>       raw PUT
                                  the whole request body is the bytes of the file itself,
                                  with no wrapper, no field name and no file name.

      This tag was made to be compatible with the way upload.php receives files; the two behave the same:

        upload.php:
          $input = fopen('php://input', 'rb');   // read the whole body directly
          fwrite($output, $buffer);              // write it to a file as is

        upload.wml:
          <upload destination="..." unique="yes" .../>

      The sender (UploadFile in Unit2.pas) already has this shape, so it needs no change:

        HttpCli.URL := 'http://localhost:8080/shop/upload.wml';
        HttpCli.ContentTypePost := MimeOf(AFileName); // ← see "Always specify Content-Type" below
        HttpCli.SendStream := Stream;
        HttpCli.RequestVer := '1.1';
        HttpCli.Put;                             // ← the key is Put, not Post

      ★ Always specify Content-Type, and it must not be application/x-www-form-urlencoded.

      This is a pitfall found in testing, with subtle symptoms: the server "receives something" and the length is exactly right,
      but the bytes at the start of the file are altered and the saved image will not open. The reason: when Indy's HTTP
      server sees application/x-www-form-urlencoded it decides the body is form text,
      and processes it as a string once before handing it to anyone, applying Windows
      best-fit character conversion on the way: bytes are treated as Latin-1 characters, and when converted to the ANSI code page,
      anything without a mapping is replaced with the "closest-looking" ASCII letter —

        FF (ÿ) → 79 ('y')      D8 (Ø) → 4F ('O')      E0 (à) → 61 ('a')

      so the JPEG header FF D8 FF E0 becomes 79 4F 79 61, while bytes below $80
      (the JFIF letters) are untouched, so it looks as if "only the first few bytes are broken".
      This happens before the server reads the request, so neither the template nor <upload> can repair it;
      only the sender can fix it by specifying the right Content-Type.

      curl's data-binary option sends application/x-www-form-urlencoded by default,
      and so does ICS's THttpCli; both must set it explicitly. image/jpeg, image/png and
      application/octet-stream all work.

      Note that the other procedure in Unit2.pas, UploadFile2, uses a multipart POST and must call
      multiupload.wml, not this page — the two procedures map to two different tags, so don't mix them up.

      How the file name is chosen: a raw PUT request carries no file name at all (only multipart does),
      so the file name must be decided on the server. Three ways:

        1. filename="a02.jpg"
           A fixed file name. Like upload.php, uploading again overwrites the same file.

        2. unique="yes" (same as nameconflict="unique"; both are recognized;
           unique matches the spelling used by <webcopy>)
           Avoids name clashes automatically: if filename is given, that name is kept, and only when a file
           of the same name already exists in the folder is _01, _02 ... appended until an unused one is found.
           Only when filename is not given is a name generated from a timestamp (yymmddhhnnsszzz).

        ★ This page writes only unique="yes", with no filename.

           Without filename, the tag first reads filename= from the Content-Disposition HTTP header,
           the standard field for passing a file name over HTTP; <webcopy>'s httpupload
           adds it automatically. So the caller needs to do nothing extra; the original file name is kept,
           and there is no need to add ?name= to the URL.

           Only when the header has no file name either (for example, curl used by hand without that header) does it fall back
           to generating one from a timestamp.

           unique="yes" handles name clashes: when a file of the same name already exists in the folder,
           _01, _02 ... is appended until the name is unique, so nothing is overwritten.

        3. Neither
           Same as unique; the extension is inferred from the request's Content-Type:
             image/jpeg .jpg    image/png .png    image/gif .gif
             image/bmp  .bmp    application/pdf .pdf
             application/zip .zip   text/plain .txt   text/csv .csv
             application/msword .doc
             application/vnd.ms-excel .xls
             application/vnd.ms-powerpoint .ppt
             ...officedocument.wordprocessingml.document .docx
             ...officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet .xlsx
             ...officedocument.presentationml.presentation .pptx
           Any unrecognized type is treated as .jpg. So the Content-Type sent must match
           the actual file type — for example, sending a PDF labeled image/jpeg makes it .jpg,
           and then header validation finds the content is a PDF that does not match .jpg, and refuses to save it.
           If you don't want to rely on inference, setting the extension with the filename attribute is the most reliable.

      Get the actual saved file name from the variable named by the savedname attribute (up_name on this page),
      because only the tag knows a generated file name, and the template needs it to show the preview.

      The file name can also come from outside, for example filename="$(name)" with ?name=... in the URL;
      the tag strips any path, blocks .. and drive letters, then checks the extension whitelist.

      Allowed types: the accept attribute lists the allowed extensions (comma-separated); empty means no restriction.
      Whatever the setting, .wml is always forbidden — if the upload folder is under the web root, a .wml would be
      "executed" by the engine, so anyone who could upload could run any template.

      In addition, "recognized formats" are checked again by the magic bytes in the file header:
        jpg/jpeg, png, gif, bmp, pdf
        zip (docx/xlsx/pptx are zip files inside, all with the PK header)
        doc/xls/ppt (Office 97-2003 OLE2 compound documents, all with the D0 CF 11 E0 header)
      This blocks "something else with a changed extension"; for example, a PDF renamed to .jpg
      is refused. Formats without a fixed header (txt, csv and so on) skip this check and are accepted.

      Opening this page directly in a browser (GET) does not cause an error; since there is no body,
      result gets 'empty body', and the page shows the usage notes below.
    -->

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="wf-head">
        <div class="wf-badge">WapForm for Web · Example</div>
        <div class="wf-title">Raw PUT Single-File Upload</div>
        <div class="wf-sub">The whole request body is one file, with no multipart wrapper — compatible with the way upload.php receives files.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">

      <upload destination="upload\"
                 accept="jpg,jpeg,png,gif,bmp,pdf,txt,csv,xlsx,docx,pptx,xls,doc,ppt,zip"
                 result="up_err"
                 size="up_size"
                 savedname="up_name"/>

      <if cnd="up_size &gt; 0">
        <div class="res-bar">
          <span>✓</span>
          <span>Saved <b>$(up_name)</b>, $(up_size) bytes.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Save result</div>
          <img class="up-preview" src="upload/$(up_name)" alt="$(up_name)"/>
        </div>
      </if>

      <if cnd="up_err &lt;> ''">
        <div class="alert">
          <span>!</span>
          <span>Nothing was saved this time: $(up_err)</span>
        </div>
      </if>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">How to test</div>
        <div class="card-note">
          This is not a form page and has no file button — a raw PUT cannot be sent from an ordinary browser form;
          a program (or curl) must send the file content directly as the request body.
        </div>

        <div class="alert">
          <span>!</span>
          <span>
            <b>Always specify Content-Type</b> when sending (for example <code>image/jpeg</code>);
            do not use the default <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> —
            it makes the server treat the file as form text: the file length looks right, but a few
            bytes are silently altered and the saved image will not open. curl's <code>--data-binary</code>
            and ICS's <code>THttpCli</code> both send that type by default, so both must set it explicitly.
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="card-note">
          <b>With Unit2.pas:</b> change the URL in UploadFile to this page and press the button.
        </div>
        <div class="code-block">
          HttpCli.URL := 'http://localhost:8080/shop/upload.wml';<br/>
          HttpCli.ContentTypePost := 'image/jpeg';<br/>
          HttpCli.SendStream := Stream;<br/>
          HttpCli.RequestVer := '1.1';<br/>
          HttpCli.Put;
        </div>

        <div class="card-note">
          <b>With curl:</b>
        </div>
        <div class="code-block">
          curl -X PUT --data-binary "@C:\Wapform\a02.jpg" -H "Content-Type: image/jpeg" http://localhost:8080/shop/upload.wml<br/>
          <br/>
          For a PDF, switch to the matching type instead of reusing image/jpeg:<br/>
          curl -X PUT --data-binary "@C:\Wapform\aaa.pdf" -H "Content-Type: application/pdf" http://localhost:8080/shop/upload.wml<br/>
          <br/>
          To set the saved file name, add a Content-Disposition header:<br/>
          curl -X PUT --data-binary "@C:\Wapform\a02.jpg" -H "Content-Type: image/jpeg" -H "Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\"a02.jpg\"" http://localhost:8080/shop/upload.wml
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="foot">
        Layout comes from <code>wapform.htm</code>; the receiving logic comes from the <code>&lt;upload&gt;</code> tag (mywap.pas).<br/>
        It is a different upload shape from <a href="multiupload.wml">File Upload</a> — that page uses a browser form's
        multipart, this one a raw PUT, and the two tags are not interchangeable.<br/>
        More examples: <a href="chart.wml">Chart Components</a>, <a href="crosstab.wml">Sales Cross Analysis</a>,
        <a href="report.wml">Grouped Sales Report</a>, <a href="function.wml">Function Self-Test</a>.
      </div>
    </div>

  </card>

</wml>
